// std::clock — the portable `Clock` pacing boundary.
//
// The corpus's time-facing canaries and the `samples/cli` pacing samples each
// declare a package-local `pub boundary trait Clock { machine
// sleep(milliseconds: u32); }` — the Win32 `Sleep` / POSIX `nanosleep` seam —
// and then require a selected provider for their own nominal trait. Provider
// selection is nominal (owning package + trait name), so bundled library code
// can never satisfy a consumer-declared trait. This module is the canonical
// `omega_language_std` `Clock` those consumers can select instead; migrating
// a local `Clock` is a one-line `use omega_language_std::clock;`.
//
// Surface: `sleep(milliseconds)` only — the sole operation every one of those
// declarations shares. Monotonic and wall-clock reads stay on `Time`
// (`time.omg` over the canonical toolchain-settled `TimeHost` seam), so this
// boundary carries no clock-read constants it cannot honor.
//
// Each selected target contributes its native sleep leaf and its provider
// default; target filtering keeps exactly one implementation closure: kernel32
// `Sleep` on windows, whose signature is the requirement's, and `nanosleep` on
// both linux targets. The linux legs do not sleep yet: `nanosleep` takes a
// `timespec*`, and nothing marshals the millisecond count into one (see the
// linux `clock_impl.omg` files).
//
// Coverage stops at `linux_x86_64`, `linux_arm64`, and `windows_x86_64`:
//
// * `macos_arm64` needs `poll(NULL, 0, ms)`, whose foreign signature differs
//   from the requirement's. A leaf's calling plan comes from its own Omega
//   signature, and the `SleepPoll` and `uses_linux_timespec_argument`
//   predicates in `host_operations.rs` have no caller in the native pipeline,
//   so no binding can supply the two leading zeros. A checked adapter over
//   the `darwin_poll` leaf is rejected: its body reaches `DarwinClock`
//   outside `Clock::sleep`'s service ceiling, and widening that ceiling would
//   leak a darwin service into every consumer's `reaches`. Both this and the
//   linux gap wait on the marshalled-syscall question in OWNER_QUESTIONS.md.
// * `macos_x86_64` is bundled but unadmitted (console's family has no
//   implementation for it either), and freestanding `uefi_x86_64` has no
//   hosted clock at all.
//
// Because the provider family does not yet cover every hosted target, this
// module is NOT listed in `main.omg`: a partial `ClockNativeProvider` family
// there would reject every consumer on the uncovered targets, even ones that
// never name `Clock`. Consumers select it with an explicit
// `use omega_language_std::clock;` — on an uncovered target the checker
// reports the family gap and names the implemented targets. Once a darwin
// realization leg lands, adding `use clock;` to `main.omg` joins it to the
// ordinary portable surface.
use targets::linux_x86_64::clock_impl;
use targets::linux_arm64::clock_impl;
use targets::windows_x86_64::clock_impl;

pub boundary trait Clock {
    // Block the caller for `milliseconds`.
    machine sleep(milliseconds: u32) reaches Clock;
}

// Nominal owner of the selected Clock provider closure. Realization leaves
// attach to this type so provider selection never assembles the row from
// unrelated implementations.
pub data ClockNativeProvider { }
